    "body": "Eli Lilly’s shares rose 4% in early morning trading after reporting revenue of $24.4 billion, well past consensus estimates.\nEli Lilly’s exponential growth can’t be stopped. The GLP-1 juggernaut once again reported an astounding year-over-year revenue increase of 39% for the third quarter, reaching $24.4 billion on the strength of tirzepatide products Mounjaro and Zepbound.\nThe results represent a sound beat of consensus, with analysts predicting revenue of $22.6 billion.\nShares of the company rose 4% in early morning trading on Thursday as investors awaited an earnings call with executives at 10 a.m. ET.\nLilly revenue growth chart\nBioSpace, Flourish\nThe massive beat was thanks to a 53% increase in volume, even as lower prices impacted sales. Key products, which included Mounjaro and Zepbound, delivered $17.1 billion in the period. Revenue from Lilly’s immunology, oncology and neuroscience key products grew 100% as compared to the year prior, according to the Thursday earnings report .\nU.S. revenue grew 34%, notching $15.1 billion as volume rose 41%. Zepbound and Mounjaro were the prime contributors here, even as the price of Zepbound declined.\nBut money also flowed in from overseas, with ex-U.S. revenue increasing 47% to $9.3 billion thanks to a 76% increase in volume. This was mainly thanks to Mounjaro, despite the drug being added to China’s National Reimbursement Drug List and its price lowered.\nPrescriptions for Lilly’s Foundayo took off in the third quarter, with the oral weight loss drug reaching more than 45,000 patients by the end of the period. As of the end of September, Lilly had snagged 40% of new patients starting an oral GLP-1 therapy, according to a corporate presentation published Thursday. The Indianapolis pharma attributed the success to broad commercial formulary and Medicare access, growing brand awareness and new launches globally. The product became available in the United Arab Emirates, Mexico, Saudi Arabia and the U.K., and is under regulatory review in more than 40 other countries.\nLilly’s incretin analogs now command 63% of the overall market, towering over rival Novo.\nEver confident, Lilly raised its full year guidance from $85-87 billion to a range of $91–92 billion.\nDiabetes\nLilly’s retatrutide trounces Novo’s Ozempic in diabetes, weight loss\nEli Lilly’s next-gen weight loss drug retatrutide soundly beat Novo’s approved medicine Ozempic in weight loss and blood sugar reduction, padding the case for a regulatory application that’s expected in the first quarter of 2027.\nOctober 28, 2026\n·\n3 min read\n·\nAnnalee Armstrong\nRead more",
